# Our Desire!
Tamil title: நமது ஆசை!

“I was killed. He stood smiling like a good man. I trusted him—and he stretched out his hand and shot me. I crumpled and fell. The bullets that flew towards my chest pecked away my life.

“I did not die; I was killed! I had no wish to die. I had even declared that I would live for 120 years. My desire was not fulfilled; I was struck down. I was prevented from living. I was killed!

“I never harboured deceit in my heart and knew nothing of duplicity. Likes and dislikes were alien to me. I had no enemies, yet I was killed. Bullets pierced me; I was shot dead.

“‘We need no discrimination; division is cruel.’ That is what I taught. Unity was my daily prayer. I harmed no one and never touched such a thought even in my mind. Yet I was killed, shot while walking, my blood scattered upon the earth.

“I said religious division was cruel. I declared that all people were equal. Hindus and Muslims were twins of one mother, the two eyes of India. They must not strike each other but live in affection. That is what I laboured for. Even my bhajan invoked ‘Ram–Rahim.’ Yet I writhed and fell, unable to withstand the gunshot. He fired; I collapsed upon my granddaughters' shoulders and died.

“‘Ram and Rahim! Reconciliation! Love! Who needs any of it?’ With the cruel thought, ‘It is empty talk and useless work; I will not spare this man,’ Godse shot and killed me. It was Godse who killed me—an ordinary human being. But what drove him to that cruel deed was religious fanaticism. Yes, religious fanaticism!”

The tomb must have told him this story. Waves of memory must have torn at his heart. The days when Gandhi spoke to him with affection must have pierced and wounded it.

“When I departed, he was here in the capital. Three years have passed since I went away. Now he is gone; only his memorial remains. I cannot meet that noble man who laboured for reconciliation. I see only his tomb. What cruelty!”

His heart must have spoken in this way. He must have been deeply moved while scattering flowers as a tribute upon the memorial. The noble man's voice rising from the tomb must have echoed throughout his heart: “Look! I preached reconciliation, and that was supposedly wrong. Religious fanaticism killed me. Here is the tomb; I am no more!”

Perhaps because these thoughts pierced his heart like spear and lance, Pakistan's Prime Minister Liaquat Ali Khan reportedly stood in silence for two minutes after placing floral wreaths as a tribute at Gandhi's memorial at Rajghat in New Delhi.

Gandhi was in a season of exhaustion, yet continued performing his duties joyfully. He was shot down. Gunfire and flying bullets are ordinary on a battlefield, but bullets struck his heart while he was walking to a public prayer meeting.

He had spoken words of affection: “There must be no religious fanaticism. It will destroy us and cause immense ruin. Let there be no hostility; why should we bear useless hatred?”

We said and laboured to prevent the country being divided into Hindustan and Pakistan. Yet the united aspiration of ten crore people became a separate country. When they demanded Pakistan, we opposed it and condemned it as a dream that could never become reality. Times changed. After witnessing with our own eyes the strength of ten crore people's ideal aspiration, even we who had opposed it accepted it. We consented to divide the country, and it was divided.

Thereafter Gandhi taught, for the country's welfare: “We must now strive to live in friendship and cultivate goodwill. We must not leap at each other or seek to intimidate. Such conduct will yield no benefit.” He condemned people becoming beasts through religious animosity. He walked courageously through a forest of corpses filled with cutting, stabbing, beating and fighting. He undertook the Noakhali pilgrimage and laboured to spread goodwill.

But religious fanaticism destroyed that noble man. It mistook a friend for an enemy and one who served the country for a traitor. In the end, it shot him down.

The dreadful hunger of the fanaticism that killed a great hero honoured by the land has still not been satisfied. It has risen crowing for more.

The country is turning into a wilderness. People are abandoning human nature and engaging in atrocities and forbidden deeds. Heart-searing scenes of sorrow have brought the prime ministers of the two countries together. India's Prime Minister Pandit Nehru and Pakistan's Prime Minister Liaquat Ali Khan are meeting to discuss how to suppress the orgy of cruelty occurring along both countries' frontiers.

Nehru and Liaquat Ali Khan are not new friends. Three years ago they moved in the same place and under the same circumstances, serving as ministers shoulder to shoulder in Delhi.

Meeting and talking is no new event for them. Liaquat and Nehru are not new friends, nor is Delhi a new place to Pakistan's Prime Minister.

Nevertheless, this meeting has aroused foreign interest and an eagerness to make predictions. London newspapers carry editorials; American newspapers publish reports with exclamation marks. The whole world watches New Delhi closely.

It is ordinary for the prime ministers of neighbouring countries to meet. Yet abroad there is a widespread feeling that something impossible is taking place.

Until three years ago the two men occupied the same place, met every day and lived in friendship. Today they meet on behalf of two countries. Their status as “prime ministers of two nations” is distinctive, but elsewhere the meeting is discussed as something extraordinary, as though Stalin and Truman were meeting.

It is ordinary, yet treated as a great event—something thought impossible is believed to be happening.

The reason is how far relations between the two countries have deteriorated within three years. Wrong assumptions, false ideas, hostile looks and angry speech prevail in two territories that until yesterday formed one country.

Pakistan is a country accepted by the Congress party, yet suspicious looks and agitated speech have not disappeared.

India is the place through which those who separated roamed until yesterday, yet their fear and furious rhetoric have not subsided.

Baseless resentments have flourished in both countries. Suspicion and trumpet calls arose over everything, creating many problems. Disputes sprouted and continue: Kashmir, trade agreements, irrigation and currency exchange. The list lengthens because of mutual distrust.

As distrust and bullying conduct persisted in both countries, many people devoted themselves to spreading rumours of an India–Pakistan war.

India and Pakistan are two significant countries in the life of Asia. Therefore growing American imperialism and Russian communism take a keen interest in events within both.

When it appears that America is planning to draw India into its net, Russia's eye turns towards Pakistan. The capitalist camp and communist bloc show great eagerness to exploit disputes between the two countries and increase support for themselves.

Just as those powers expected, people within both countries issued warnings. Newspapers here wrote fiery demands to “suppress Pakistan's arrogance,” and Pakistan's propaganda cannon thundered in reply. Foreign powers that planned to exploit dissatisfaction in the two new nations to expand their dominance now watch New Delhi without taking their eyes away.

News that Pakistan's Prime Minister Liaquat Ali Khan arrived from Karachi, that India's Prime Minister Nehru received him, that Liaquat paid homage at the memorial of the noble Gandhi, and that the talks are proceeding satisfactorily falls upon us like drops of honey.

We have always condemned religious fanaticism and the growth of resentment that follows it.

The resentment that grew then continues even now. Fanatic conduct plays not only in India but in Pakistan as well.

Gandhi himself opposed the partition creating Pakistan. Later, however, he accepted that the demand contained justice and that denying it would turn the country into a field of blood. Two countries came into being.

The prime ministers meet seeking an end to the destructive course born of religious fanaticism. Reports inspire hope that the talks will succeed. We are happy. Our desire is that the two countries live as mutual friends and walk a good path. A sign of that future has dawned.

Standing silently before Gandhi's tomb, the heart of Pakistan's Prime Minister must have trembled at religious fanaticism's terrible dance.

When receiving Pakistan's Prime Minister, Nehru—India's Prime Minister—may have rejoiced at the thought that the disputes would end. This new condition, now a bud, must become a flower and turn fanatical hearts towards the path of affection.

Some people in both countries believe “Pakistan is our enemy”; hostility has not changed. They seek to satisfy their desires through provocative acts. The dreadful scenes occurring in Bengal are the result. Fanatics who have pawned their intelligence and become slaves to anger must be restrained.

To end their arrogance, the rulers must reach a united decision. The people of both countries, and the newspapers that reflect public opinion, must cooperate fully in implementing it.

Unfortunately, the conduct of leading newspapers in both countries has still not changed.

“Demons!”

“Uncivilised people!”

The newspapers of each country use such ugly words for the other's leaders. They behave as though abuse were their sacred duty.

False reports, sudden rumours and inflammatory headlines perform a frenzied dance that incites fanatics.

This must disappear immediately. The conviction must grow that the two countries can live only through unity, and that the people must be mobilised along that path.

If resentment grows, religious fanaticism will raise its head. Gandhi, the jewel of the nation who ought to be among us, fell victim to its dreadful dance and lies in a tomb—as though continuing to teach, “There must be no religious fanaticism; it will cause immense destruction.”

That memory must stir the hearts of nationalist newspapers and all who cultivate resentment.

The two countries must join hands and live as friends. That is what is good for both.

(*Dravida Nadu*, 9 April 1950)
